  <h2 id="what-is">What Is a Website Content Audit</h2>
  <p>A website content audit evaluates each page against its intended task, current accuracy, search visibility, user behaviour, conversions and earned links. The output is a decision log that explains whether to keep, update, combine, redirect or remove each URL.</p>
  <p>Unlike a technical audit that examines infrastructure, a content audit focuses on the purpose, accuracy and measured value of words, images and media. Its inventory is limited by the sources and settings used, so reconcile crawl, sitemap, platform and search data before making page decisions.</p>
  <p>Most websites accumulate content over years without ever reviewing it. Pages become outdated, topics overlap, and thin content dilutes your site's topical authority. A content audit brings order to this chaos.</p>

  <h2 id="why-matters">Why Content Audits Matter</h2>
  <p>A content audit tests whether each page has a useful current purpose and evidence that supports keeping or changing it. Search and analytics metrics inform the decision but do not replace editorial or business context.</p>
  <p>Here's what a content audit reveals:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pages without measured activity:</strong> verify index status, tracking, seasonality, conversion role, legal need and backlinks before choosing an action. No traffic alone does not justify removal.</li>
    <li><strong>Query overlap:</strong> several URLs can appear for related queries without harming each other. Investigate whether pages duplicate the same task or swap positions before consolidating.</li>
    <li><strong>Content gaps:</strong> Topics your audience searches for but you haven't covered. These are opportunities to create new content that fills a genuine need.</li>
    <li><strong>Change over time:</strong> compare clicks and impressions across equivalent periods. Investigate demand, result layout, technical changes and content accuracy before calling a decline content decay.</li>
    <li><strong>Conversion opportunities:</strong> review high-traffic pages with weak task completion or conversion evidence. Estimate value from the site's own data and test the proposed change.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-to-evaluate">What to Evaluate</h2>
  <p>A thorough content audit examines each page across several dimensions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Traffic:</strong> How much organic and total traffic does the page receive? Is it trending up or down?</li>
    <li><strong>Search visibility:</strong> which queries produce impressions and clicks for the page, and how has the pattern changed across equivalent periods?</li>
    <li><strong>Engagement:</strong> Bounce rate, time on page, scroll depth. Are visitors actually reading the content?</li>
    <li><strong>Backlinks:</strong> How many external sites link to this page? High-value backlink pages should be preserved even if traffic is low.</li>
    <li><strong>Conversions:</strong> Does the page contribute to your goals — leads, sales, signups?</li>
    <li><strong>Freshness:</strong> When was the content last updated? Is the information still accurate?</li>
    <li><strong>Quality:</strong> Is the content comprehensive, well-written, and genuinely useful? Does it demonstrate E-E-A-T (Experience, Expertise, Authoritativeness, Trust)?</li>
    <li><strong>Technical:</strong> Does the page have proper meta tags, schema markup, internal links, and optimised images?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="process">Content Audit Process</h2>
  <p>Use this decision map to scope the work. The <a href="/website-content-audit/how-to/">six-step content audit guide</a> contains the operational spreadsheet and execution procedure.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Define the inventory:</strong> reconcile reachable, submitted, known and search-visible URLs and document gaps.</li>
    <li><strong>Attach evidence:</strong> join available search, analytics, conversion, link, accuracy and ownership evidence using declared periods.</li>
    <li><strong>Review purpose:</strong> decide whether each page still supports a distinct user and business task before applying a score.</li>
    <li><strong>Assign and verify:</strong> record the action, rationale, owner, dependencies and success check; require manual review before deletion, consolidation or redirect.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="scoring">Content Scoring Framework</h2>
  <p>A useful score must be calibrated to the site's scale and goals. The example matrix describes evidence states rather than universal traffic or link thresholds:</p>
  <div role="region" aria-label="Content audit evidence matrix" tabindex="0">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Dimension</th>
        <th>Limited evidence</th>
        <th>Mixed evidence</th>
        <th>Strong evidence</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Traffic</td>
        <td>No comparable data</td>
        <td>Below site baseline</td>
        <td>Supports the page's goal</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Quality</td>
        <td>Task or accuracy unclear</td>
        <td>Useful with known gaps</td>
        <td>Current and task-complete</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Backlinks</td>
        <td>Link data unavailable</td>
        <td>Some relevant links</td>
        <td>Important relevant links</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Conversions</td>
        <td>No contribution</td>
        <td>Assists conversions</td>
        <td>Directly converts</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>Document how each dimension is weighted and why. A low composite score should trigger review rather than automatically trigger removal.</p>

  <h2 id="tools">Tools for Content Audits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Screaming Frog:</strong> exports metadata for URLs reached through its configured seeds, links, sitemaps and settings. Reconcile the crawl with other sources rather than treating it as the entire site.</li>
    <li><strong>Google Search Console:</strong> Provides real keyword data — what queries bring traffic to each page, click-through rates, and average positions.</li>
    <li><strong>Google Analytics:</strong> Traffic, engagement metrics, and conversion data per page. Essential for scoring content performance.</li>
    <li><strong>Ahrefs Content Explorer:</strong> Shows backlink data and content performance metrics. Useful for identifying high-value pages worth preserving.</li>
    <li><strong>Semrush Content Audit Tool:</strong> Automates much of the process by pulling data from multiple sources into a single dashboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Spreadsheet software:</strong> Organise raw URL evidence and assigned actions in a structure your team can maintain. Our <a href="/website-content-audit/template/">template page</a> provides blank Markdown worksheets, not a prebuilt spreadsheet.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="actions">What to Do With Your Findings</h2>
  <p>The action field records the reviewed decision; the score is supporting triage evidence, not the decision rule. Use the <a href="/website-content-audit/how-to/#assign-actions">operational guide</a> to execute and monitor each action.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Keep:</strong> High-performing content that's accurate and current. No changes needed beyond routine monitoring.</li>
    <li><strong>Update:</strong> Content with potential that needs refreshing — new data, better examples, expanded sections, improved formatting.</li>
    <li><strong>Consolidate:</strong> Multiple thin pages on the same topic merged into one comprehensive resource. Redirect the old URLs to the new page.</li>
    <li><strong>Redirect:</strong> use when a page has a clear relevant successor and the old URL should no longer stand alone. Preserve useful content and links in the destination.</li>
    <li><strong>Remove:</strong> use when the page has no continuing user, business, legal or search purpose. Return an appropriate status or redirect only when a relevant destination exists.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The safest decision is evidence-led and reversible where possible. Record why a URL changes, preserve material content and links and monitor the intended destination after release.</p>
